Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 14:16:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] Bluetooth: MGMT: stop and account mesh transmissions correctly

A mesh packet is advertised through a dedicated advertising instance
(le_num_of_adv_sets + 1). Since the list_empty() check was added to
mesh_send_done_sync(), that instance is what keeps hdev->adv_instances
non-empty, so a mesh transmission never disables advertising when its
count is done or when it is cancelled: on the legacy path the controller
keeps advertising the packet and the instance stays scheduled for
1000 s, with extended advertising the set stays enabled for the duration
the controller was given. The tools/mesh-tester cases "Mesh - Send
cancel - 1/2" encode the original behaviour (the end of advertising
after the cancel) and time out on current kernels.

Independently, mesh_send_done_sync() completes the head of the pending
queue rather than the request that held the advertising instance, so
after cancelling the in-flight handle the next, still unsent, packet is
reported complete without having been advertised.

Tearing the instance down means controller commands inside
mesh_send_done_sync(), and that work clears HCI_MESH_SENDING first: a
Mesh Send arriving while it waits for the controller sees the flag
clear, queues a start, and the destroy callback mesh_next() queues the
head of the queue again afterwards - the same packet is started twice.
The window exists today in the microseconds between the clear and
mesh_next(); the tear-down would widen it to a controller round trip.
Patch 1 therefore comes first: it keeps the flag set until the next
packet has been queued or the scheduler goes idle, and makes that
decision under hdev->lock, where mesh_send() tests the flag, without
holding the lock across a controller command. Patch 2 tears the mesh
instance down through hci_remove_advertising_sync() on both advertising
paths - that set only with extended advertising, so other advertisers
keep running - and keeps Advertising Removed from being emitted for the
internal instance. Patch 3 completes the request that acquired the
instance.

Patch 1 touches send_cancel() as 71af682ba469 ("Bluetooth: mgmt:
Dequeue pending mesh_send_sync entries on cancel") left it; stable
trees need that commit first, and patch 1 lists it as a prerequisite.
With it, the three patches apply cumulatively to the current
linux-7.2.y, 6.18.y, 6.12.y, 6.6.y and 6.1.y tips; no stable kernel was
built or run.

Tested with tools/test-runner (qemu) on the bluetooth tree, with
tools/mesh-tester extended by the BlueZ patch "tools/mesh-tester: Test
mesh advertising lifecycle" (BREDRLE50 variants of the Send/cancel
cases, and lifecycle cases that check the Mesh Packet Complete handles,
which request each start of the mesh set belongs to, how often the set
is torn down, that no Advertising Removed is sent for the mesh instance,
that the ordinary advertising set is left alone, and what Read
Advertising Features lists afterwards; one case holds the tear-down
command in the emulator and sends a packet while the kernel waits for
it):

  mesh-tester, 25 cases, KVM:                 unpatched 10/25, patched 25/25
  mesh-tester, 20 Send cases, TCG + valgrind: unpatched  5/20, patched 20/20
  the cancel cases and the hold case, 5 runs: patched 60/60
  mgmt-tester, 501 cases, patched:            501/501

In the recorded runs, the series disabled and removed the mesh set after
the requested mesh-done interval, while the ordinary extended-advertising
set remained enabled. The cnt=3 cases use a nominal 75 ms interval. The
legacy coexistence case checks preservation of the ordinary advertiser
only; mesh transmission while another legacy instance is rotating remains
a separate pre-existing limitation. The TCG/valgrind run passed all 20
functional cases but reported seven tester-side errors, also present with
the unpatched kernel. The original generic cancel cases remain
load-sensitive. With only the tear-down applied (patches 2 and 3 without
patch 1) the hold case starts the second packet twice on both emulators;
with patch 1 it is started once.

This series fixes normal and cancellation-driven mesh-done teardown.
Recovery after advertising setup/removal failure and all pending-request
behavior across power transitions are not established by these tests.

Iaroslav Voitovych (3):
  Bluetooth: MGMT: hand mesh transmissions over under hdev->lock
  Bluetooth: MGMT: remove the mesh advertising instance when done
  Bluetooth: MGMT: complete the mesh transmission that owned the instance

